def intersection(interval1, interval2):
    # Calculate the start and end of the intersection interval
    start = max(interval1[0], interval2[0])
    end = min(interval1[1], interval2[1])

    # Check if there is an intersection
    if start > end:
        return "NO"

    # Calculate the length of the intersection
    intersection_length = end - start + 1

    # Check if the intersection length is a prime number
    if intersection_length <= 1:
        return "NO"
    for i in range(2, int(intersection_length ** 0.5) + 1):
        if intersection_length % i == 0:
            return "NO"

    return "YES"
```

This solution first calculates the start and end of the intersection interval. If the calculated start is greater than the end, there is no intersection, so it returns "NO". Otherwise, it calculates the length of the intersection and checks if this length is a prime number by testing divisibility from 2 up to the square root of the length. If any divisor is found, it returns "NO", otherwise, it returns "YES".

